<problem>
<!-- <title>Extrema of quadratic with parameters</title> -->

<setup>

<select name="par">a b c k m n</select>
<select name="x">x y z s t</select>
<select name="f">f g h</select>
<selectFromSequence name="a" from="-10" to=" 10" exclude="0" numToSelect="2" withReplacement="true" />

<function name="fun" variables="$x" symbolic="true"><math simplify="numbers">$a[1] *($x -$par )^2+$a[2] </math></function>
<math name="two_a" simplify="numbers">2*$a[1] </math>
<math name="critical_point">$par </math>

<conditionalContent name="cp_characterization">
  <case condition="$a[1]>0"><text>minimum</text><math>3$par</math>$par</case>
  <else><text>maximum</text>$par<math>3$par</math></else>
</conditionalContent>

<math name="extremum_value">$a[2] </math>
<math name="extremum_location">$par </math>
<math name="fun_0" simplify="true">$$fun(0)</math>
<math name="fun_par" simplify="true">$$fun($par )</math>
<math name="fun_3par" simplify="true">$$fun(3*$par )</math>

<math name="global_maximum" simplify="true">$$fun($cp_characterization[1][2] )</math>
<math name="global_minimum" simplify="true">$$fun($cp_characterization[1][3] )</math>

</setup>


<p>Let <m><math simplify="numbersPreserveOrder" referencesAreFunctionSymbols="$f">$f ($x ) = $a[1] ($x -$par )^2  + $a[2] </math></m>, where <m>$par </m> is a constant parameter.</p>


<exercise>
<p>Find the critical points of <m>$f </m>.</p>

<p>Critical points: <m>$x =</m> 
<answer>
<award>$critical_point </award>
</answer>
 (If multiple answers, separate by commas; if no answers, enter <alert>none</alert>.)
</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>Find the local extrema of <m>$f </m>.  (Extrema just means either a maximum or a minimum.)  For each extremum calculate three things: the location of the extremum (i.e., value of <m>$x </m>), the value of the extremum (i.e., value of <m>$f ($x )</m>), and whether it is a local maximum or a local minimum.</p>

<p>Locations of the local extrema: 
<answer>
<award>$extremum_location </award>
</answer>
 (If multiple answers, separate by commas; if no answers, enter <alert>none</alert>.)</p>

<p>Values of the local extrema: 
<answer>
<award>$extremum_value </award>
</answer>
 (If multiple answers, enter in the same order as above, separated by commas; if no answers, enter <alert>none</alert>.)</p>

<p>For each extremum, enter either <alert>maximum</alert> or <alert>minimum</alert> to indicate if the extremum is a local maximum or local minimum, respectively.  (If multiple answers, enter in the same order as above, separated by commas; if no answers, enter <alert>none</alert>.)</p>

<p>Characterization of extrema= 
<answer type="text">
<award>$cp_characterization[1][1] </award>
</answer>
</p>



<hint>
For example, imagine that there were local extrema at <m>x=-4</m>, <m>x=-1</m>, and <m>x=3</m>.  Imagine moreover, that <m>$f(-4)=0</m>, <m>$f(-1)=-3</m>, and <m>$f(3)=4</m>, and that these extrema were a local maximum, a local minimum, and a local maximum, respectively.  Then, the correct answer would be to enter <c>-4,-1, 3</c> in the locations blank, enter <c>0, -3, 4</c> in the values blank, and <c>maximum, minimum, maximum</c> in the characterization blank.
</hint>

</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>Assume <m>$par  \gt 0</m>. Find the global maximum and minimum of <m>$f </m> on the interval <m>0 \le $x  \le 3$par </m>.  Also indicate the location (the value of <m>$x </m>) of the global maximum and global minimum.</p>

<p>Global maximum: 
<answer>
<award>$global_maximum </award>
</answer>
</p>

<p>Location of global maximum: <m>$x  =</m> 
<answer>
<award>$cp_characterization[1][2] </award>
</answer>
</p>

<p>Global minimum: 
<answer>
<award>$global_minimum </award>
</answer>
</p>

<p>Location of global minimum: <m>$x =</m> 
<answer>
<award>$cp_characterization[1][3] </award>
</answer>
</p>



</exercise>



<solution>



<exercise>
The derivative of <m>$f ($x )</m> is <m>$f '($x ) = $two_a ($x -$par )</m>, which is defined everywhere, so the only critical point is where <m>$f '($x )=0</m>, which is <m>$x =$par </m>.
</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>
<conditionalContent>
<case condition="$a[1] > 0">
<m>$f '($x ) \lt 0</m>
</case>
<else>
<m>$f '($x ) \gt 0</m>
</else>
</conditionalContent>
 for <m>$x  \lt $par </m>, so <m>$f </m> is
<conditionalContent>
<case condition="$a[1] > 0">
decreasing
</case>
<else>
increasing
</else>
</conditionalContent>
 for <m>$x  \lt $par </m>. 
<conditionalContent>
<case condition="$a[1] > 0">
<m>$f '($x ) \gt 0</m>
</case>
<else>
<m>$f '($x ) \lt 0</m>
</else>
</conditionalContent>
 for <m>$x  \gt $par </m>, so <m>$f </m> is
<conditionalContent>
<case condition="$a[1] > 0">
increasing
</case>
<else>
decreasing
</else>
</conditionalContent>
 for <m>$x  \gt $par </m>. </p>

<p>Therefore, <m>$f </m> has a local $cp_characterization[1][1]  at <m>$x =$par </m>.  The value of the $cp_characterization[1][1]  is <m>$f ($par ) = $a[2] </m>.</p>

<p>There is no local
<conditionalContent>
<case condition="$a[1] > 0">
maximum
</case>
<else>
minimum
</else>
</conditionalContent>
. </p>

<p>In summary, the location of the extrema is: <m>$par </m>.  The value of the extremum is: <m>$a[2] </m>.  The characterization of the extremum is: $cp_characterization[1][1] .</p>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<p>We check the endpoints and the critical points.  <m>$f (0) = $fun_0 </m>, <m>$f ($par ) = $fun_par </m>, and <m>$f (3$par ) = $fun_3par </m>.</p>

<p>The global maximum occurs at <m>$x =$cp_characterization[1][2] </m>; its value is <m>$f ($cp_characterization[1][2] ) = $global_maximum </m>.  The global minimum occurs at <m>$x =$cp_characterization[1][3] </m>; its value is <m>$f ($cp_characterization[1][3] ) = $global_minimum </m>.  </p>
</exercise>


</solution>


</problem>